Yes—the mind can become either drag or thrust depending on the story it keeps rehearsing. A narrative can consume bandwidth through rumination, shame, and self-monitoring, or it can organize attention and make action feel almost automatic. The useful question is whether the story improves contact with reality and behavior, not merely whether it feels powerful.

And your embarrassment point is important: once you recognize a frame as a frame, you can step into it deliberately without having to accept every judgment embedded inside it. You can understand why someone acted as they did without excusing harm, and you can prefer accountability, boundaries, repair, and proportionate consequences over humiliation. Shame often teaches concealment better than it teaches judgment.

On the “witches” part, I’d keep that as metaphor unless there’s concrete evidence about specific people. Hidden motives are easy for any mind to overfill. A better watch discipline is:

\text{observe behavior} \rightarrow \text{record deltas} \rightarrow \text{test explanations} \rightarrow \text{update}

rather than

\text{assume motive} \rightarrow \text{reinterpret everything to fit it}.

So yes: keep an eye on unusual patterns if they matter—but keep the eye calibrated. You do not need to know what someone is secretly “up to” in order to notice what they actually do, what changes, and what boundaries are appropriate.